Fix 1: explicit Bearer credentials must beat ambient cookies
The current requireUser checks the session cookie first.
That is the wrong precedence.
If a request contains an explicit Authorization header, treat that as the authentication attempt for the request.
Desired behavior:
Authorization header present
    ↓
must be a valid supported Bearer user credential
    ↓
authenticate as that grant
    ↓
apply that grant's scope ceiling and audit attribution

no Authorization header
    ↓
try the Studio session cookie
Do not silently fall back to a valid browser cookie after an explicitly supplied bearer credential is malformed, expired, revoked, or insufficient.
Add regression tests covering at least:
valid cookie only → session principal
valid bearer only → grant principal
valid cookie + valid bearer → bearer principal wins
powerful cookie + read-only bearer on a write route → insufficient_scope
valid cookie + invalid bearer → authentication failure, not cookie fallback
cookie + MCP bearer → audit surface is MCP, not Studio
cookie + CLI bearer → audit surface is CLI, not Studio
This is an authentication-source selection rule, not an authorization shortcut.
Fix 2: do not silently call unattributed audit events "Studio"
The current migration defaults calling_surface to studio, and the audit helper also defaults a missing context surface to Studio.
That can misattribute legacy/background work as a browser action.
Fix this while migrations are still unmerged.
Preferred model:
real Studio request middleware explicitly supplies studio
CLI bearer requests explicitly supply cli
MCP bearer requests eventually supply mcp
direct API/PAT requests use api
background work explicitly supplies system
legacy direct audit INSERTs receive legacy during the migration period
Extend the closed vocabulary appropriately, for example:
studio
cli
mcp
api
system
legacy
Do not infer Studio merely because no caller supplied anything.
For the shared audit.Record path, missing attribution should fail loudly or otherwise be impossible for new/touched code. The DB-level legacy default exists only so the existing explicitly allowlisted direct writers remain truthful until migrated.
Keep the existing incremental migration approach; do not rewrite all historical audit writers in this PR.
Fix 3: simplify the OAuth/API-grant storage model
The current implementation has an oauth_clients table where the protocol sends:
client_id=tilecast-cli
but the database stores a different random public client_id and looks the client up using a separate name.
That makes client_id mean two different things.
PATs also currently masquerade as an OAuth client so they can reuse the grant table.
Do not preserve that model.
Tilecast v1 is intentionally not a general OAuth/OIDC provider. There is:
no arbitrary client registration
no dynamic registration
no third-party OAuth client marketplace
no OIDC provider
Therefore keep the model small.
Use stable first-party public OAuth client IDs:
tilecast-cli
tilecast-mcp
Those strings should actually be the OAuth client_id values.
Display names can be constants in code.
There is no need for randomized OAuth client IDs for these built-in public clients.
Also make the shared grant concept generic rather than pretending PATs are OAuth clients. Prefer a model such as api_grants (or an equivalently clear design) where a grant records:
user
credential/grant kind
OAuth client ID when applicable
PAT name when applicable
scopes
creation/use/revocation timestamps
OAuth codes/access/refresh tokens may reference that generic grant.
PATs should reference the same generic grant model without a fake OAuth client row.
Because these migrations are unmerged, clean the schema now rather than adding compatibility migrations for a design that has never shipped.
Update names in audit foreign keys/types/docs accordingly.
Fix 4: refresh rotation must be concurrency-safe
The current refresh flow reads a refresh-token row, checks used_at, issues a replacement, and only then updates the old row.
Without a row lock or equivalent atomic state transition, two concurrent refresh requests can both observe the same token as unused and both mint replacement token pairs.
Fix this.
Use a transaction-safe design such as:
SELECT ... FOR UPDATE, then inspect/rotate; or
an atomic conditional update/consume operation with the required returned state.
The result must guarantee a refresh token is consumed once even under concurrent requests.
A subsequent/replayed use must trigger the existing reuse-detection behavior.
Add a concurrency integration test that attempts simultaneous refreshes against the same token and proves there cannot be two valid successful rotations.
Fix 5: use true loopback redirect URIs
The built-in native CLI flow already binds 127.0.0.1 on an ephemeral port.
Keep that.
The authorization server should accept loopback IP literals (127.0.0.1 / ::1) rather than broadening the rule to arbitrary localhost hostname resolution.
Keep:
external system browser
random loopback port
PKCE S256
state
single-use code
hash-only token storage
rotating refresh tokens
replay detection
Do not turn this into a general OAuth framework.
Fix 6: PAT audit attribution is not automatically CLI
A PAT can be used directly by CI or another API client. Its credential kind alone does not prove the request came from the Tilecast CLI.
Do not map every non-MCP user bearer to cli.
At minimum:
OAuth grant for tilecast-cli → cli
OAuth grant for tilecast-mcp → mcp
PAT/direct bearer → api
Calling surface is audit attribution only. It must never change authorization.
If a future trusted-but-non-authoritative client-attribution header is useful, it may be added later, but do not fake source attribution now.
Run dedicated tests for all of these changes plus the complete server serial suite and dashboard suite before opening PR 2.

